# The same refusal on the float side, and the same reason it is worth pinning:
# `to_float` takes bytes as happily as it takes a string, and said neither.
#
# A `none` is the argument because it renders without a module name, so the
# direct run and the run through an import say the same words.
import "std/text"

# A none the check cannot see. A call handing a provable none to a group with
# no `none` arm is refused at check, so the runtime sentence below is reached
# through a closure parameter, which the check does not look into.
fn unseen f
  f 0

pub play = print "{text/to_float (unseen (_ -> none))}"
